1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,534
When you type good restaurants near me into your

2
00:00:02,534 --> 00:00:05,068
phone, how does a machine that only understands numbers

3
00:00:05,068 --> 00:00:07,602
know what you mean? The field that makes this

4
00:00:07,602 --> 00:00:10,136
possible is called natural language processing, or NLP. By

5
00:00:10,136 --> 00:00:12,669
the end of this video, you will know how

6
00:00:12,669 --> 00:00:15,203
a computer turns your words into something it can

7
00:00:15,203 --> 00:00:15,766
work with.

8
00:00:16,166 --> 00:00:18,860
Imagine you receive a letter in a language you

9
00:00:18,860 --> 00:00:21,553
do not know. You hire a translator who breaks

10
00:00:21,553 --> 00:00:24,247
the letter into small pieces, looks each piece up

11
00:00:24,247 --> 00:00:26,940
in a dictionary, and then works out what the

12
00:00:26,940 --> 00:00:29,634
whole letter is saying. NLP works in a similar

13
00:00:29,634 --> 00:00:32,327
way. A computer cannot read words the way we

14
00:00:32,327 --> 00:00:35,021
do, so natural language processing turns language into pieces

15
00:00:35,021 --> 00:00:37,714
and numbers it can work with, step by step.

16
00:00:38,114 --> 00:00:41,000
Step one is tokenization. The computer splits your sentence

17
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:43,885
into small pieces called tokens. A token is often

18
00:00:43,885 --> 00:00:46,770
a whole word, but it can also be part

19
00:00:46,770 --> 00:00:49,656
of a word or a punctuation mark. Then each

20
00:00:49,656 --> 00:00:52,541
token is swapped for a number, because computers work

21
00:00:52,541 --> 00:00:55,426
with numbers, not letters. The numbers on screen are

22
00:00:55,426 --> 00:00:57,991
only examples. Every system has its own numbering.

23
00:00:58,391 --> 00:01:01,081
A number alone does not tell the computer what

24
00:01:01,081 --> 00:01:03,771
a word means. So the next step gives each

25
00:01:03,771 --> 00:01:06,461
token a long list of numbers, called an embedding.

26
00:01:06,461 --> 00:01:09,151
You can think of an embedding as a position

27
00:01:09,151 --> 00:01:11,840
on a map of meaning. Words used in similar

28
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:14,530
ways end up close together: cat near dog, car

29
00:01:14,530 --> 00:01:17,220
near truck, pizza near rice. A real map has

30
00:01:17,220 --> 00:01:19,910
hundreds or thousands of dimensions, but the idea is

31
00:01:19,910 --> 00:01:21,704
the same. Closeness means similar use.

32
00:01:22,104 --> 00:01:24,942
Step three is learning. A model is trained on

33
00:01:24,942 --> 00:01:27,781
huge amounts of text. It learns patterns, such as

34
00:01:27,781 --> 00:01:30,620
which words tend to appear together, and how word

35
00:01:30,620 --> 00:01:31,566
order changes meaning.

36
00:01:31,966 --> 00:01:34,946
Once trained, the model handles new text in the

37
00:01:34,946 --> 00:01:37,926
same steps. When you give it new text, your

38
00:01:37,926 --> 00:01:40,906
words become tokens. The tokens become numbers. The model

39
00:01:40,906 --> 00:01:43,887
works with those numbers. And then it produces an

40
00:01:43,887 --> 00:01:46,867
answer, such as a translation, a label, or a

41
00:01:46,867 --> 00:01:47,198
reply.

42
00:01:47,598 --> 00:01:50,460
You use NLP every day. Search engines use it

43
00:01:50,460 --> 00:01:53,321
to understand what you are looking for. Your email

44
00:01:53,321 --> 00:01:56,183
uses it to filter spam. Phones use it to

45
00:01:56,183 --> 00:01:59,044
autocomplete messages and to turn speech into text. Translation

46
00:01:59,044 --> 00:02:01,588
tools and chatbots are built on it too.

47
00:02:01,988 --> 00:02:04,966
Language is hard for computers. The word bank can

48
00:02:04,966 --> 00:02:07,944
mean the side of a river or a place

49
00:02:07,944 --> 00:02:10,922
for money, and only the context tells you which.

50
00:02:10,922 --> 00:02:13,900
The sentence, I saw her duck, could be about

51
00:02:13,900 --> 00:02:16,878
a bird or about someone bending down. Sarcasm and

52
00:02:16,878 --> 00:02:19,856
slang trip up even the best systems. And languages

53
00:02:19,856 --> 00:02:22,834
with less text available online, often including many African

54
00:02:22,834 --> 00:02:25,150
languages, usually get weaker tools than English.

55
00:02:25,550 --> 00:02:28,113
So does a computer truly understand language the way

56
00:02:28,113 --> 00:02:30,675
you do? Researchers still debate that. What we know

57
00:02:30,675 --> 00:02:33,238
is that these systems are very good at finding

58
00:02:33,238 --> 00:02:35,801
patterns in text, and that is often enough to

59
00:02:35,801 --> 00:02:36,370
be useful.

60
00:02:36,770 --> 00:02:40,341
Let's recap. One: NLP helps computers work with human

61
00:02:40,341 --> 00:02:43,911
language by turning it into numbers. Two: the steps

62
00:02:43,911 --> 00:02:47,482
are tokens, then embeddings, then a trained model that

63
00:02:47,482 --> 00:02:51,052
finds patterns. Three: you already use it in search,

64
00:02:51,052 --> 00:02:53,036
translation, spam filters, and chatbots.

65
00:02:53,436 --> 00:02:56,329
Modern chatbots take these ideas much further. In the

66
00:02:56,329 --> 00:02:59,222
next video, we look at how large language models

67
00:02:59,222 --> 00:03:02,115
predict the next word, and why that idea is

68
00:03:02,115 --> 00:03:05,008
so powerful. If this helped, subscribe, and comment with

69
